\title{Quantifier restriction}
\date{2026-02-05T15:13:04Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{We naturally make "for all" and "exists" statements in English about adjectives applied to sets, like "all even numbers" or "there exists a prime, happy number greater than 17".  The most natural way to encode such statements is by making up a domain to represent the adjectives, like "#{\all{n}{\Even} \dots}" where #{\Even} represents the set of even numbers, or "#{\ext{n}{H}}" where #{H} is the set of prime, happy numbers greater than 17.  However, when we hide all the conditions inside the definition of a domain, we cannot work with those conditions using the tools of propositional logic we have developed.  Therefore, we are going to learn how to translate statements involving complicated domains into logically equivalent statements with simpler, standard domains.}

\transclude{00HR}    % Forall
\transclude{00HS}    % Exists
\transclude{00HT}    % Note forall vs exists, memorize?
